Wave 1 is dual-sourced

A reader who wants to reproduce the record behind Table 1 needs to know, for each wave, where the numbers come from and whether they can be checked against a single source. For three of the four drafting waves the answer is straightforward. For wave 1 it is not, which is why this wave gets its own section: a reproducer's confidence in the table depends on knowing exactly which rows deserve extra care and why. This section explains how the wave 1 record is put together, where each number comes from, why the two available sources disagree, why the disagreement cannot be settled after the fact, and why nothing in the paper depends on settling it.

The wave itself is small. Wave 1 consists of two briefs, a paper outline and a related-work draft. Table 1 reports four drafting waves in total, and wave 1 is the only wave whose produced-byte count has two sources, and the only one where those sources disagree. Dual-sourced here means two independent counts of the same quantity, not two copies of the same count that happen to sit in different places. Every other wave carries a single count with a single provenance. Wave 1 carries two counts, taken from two vantage points on the same work, and they differ by roughly a factor of eight.

The first source is the dispatch instrument. It logged 1,397 bytes for the outline brief and 1,438 bytes for the related-work brief, which together give 2,835 first-pass bytes for the wave. Both figures sit in the public instrument table, at rows 1 and 2, so a reproducer can read them directly from the instrument's own record without passing through the paper. Because the rest of the record reproduces from this instrument, these two numbers are the natural starting point for any recheck.

The second source is the hub's own audit, taken at weave time. The audit counted 22,824 bytes produced for wave 1 and 3,853 characters admitted, and on those counts it yields a hub-audited admitted fraction of 0.17. This count was made independently of the dispatch instrument: the hub audited the wave when its outputs were woven and produced its own totals rather than inheriting the instrument's.

The two sources disagree, and not by a rounding error. The instrument records 2,835 first-pass bytes for the wave. The hub audit records 22,824 bytes produced. That is a gap of about a factor of eight. Each count could be internally consistent with how it was taken and they could still disagree, because they were taken from different vantage points, and the record cannot say which one tracks what was actually produced. There is no way to find out, because the raw worker outputs for wave 1 were not retained. This is the most important fact in this section: the artefacts that would let a reproducer reconcile the two counts, byte by byte, no longer exist. Reconciliation would mean going back over the wave's actual outputs and attributing their size to one source or the other. With those outputs gone, any attempt to close the gap would be guesswork, and the record does not guess.

The paper handles the disagreement by declining to pick a winner. Table 1 prints both figures for wave 1, with a note marking the row as dual-sourced. Neither number is dropped, and neither is presented as a correction of the other. A reader who opens Table 1 expecting one produced-byte count for the first wave and finds two is looking at the intended presentation, not at a typo.

There is a second consequence, and it affects anyone who tries to recompute the admitted fraction from the instrument side. For these two rows the admitted fraction is undefined on the instrument denominator. The reason is arithmetic: the brief sizes, 1,615 and 1,741 bytes for the two rows, exceed the logged transcripts of 1,397 and 1,438 bytes. A fraction computed on that basis is not meaningful, so the paper reports it as undefined rather than printing a number the underlying record cannot support. A reproducer who runs this computation should expect the same undefined result and should not read it as a mistake in their own working.

The natural worry at this point is that one unverifiable wave contaminates everything downstream of it. It does not, and the paper is built so that it cannot. Nothing in the cost model or the anatomy depends on wave 1 alone. The free-lane corollary needs only that the acceptance probability is above a small threshold, which does not turn on the exact produced-byte count of any single wave. The per-context-budget bound is stated on the pooled admitted fraction and on the per-wave admitted fractions, and it does not reduce to wave 1's contested figures. The contested numbers are load-bearing for the record's honesty, not for its results. The remaining waves reproduce cleanly: waves 2 to 4 reproduce from the instrument, with wave 3 reproducing exactly. The bulk of the quantitative record can therefore be verified without touching the disputed wave at all.

For a reproducer working through the record in order, the sequence is short. Read rows 1 and 2 of the public instrument table and confirm 1,397 bytes and 1,438 bytes, summing to 2,835 first-pass bytes for the wave. Take the hub audit's weave-time figures, 22,824 bytes produced and 3,853 characters admitted, a hub-audited admitted fraction of 0.17, and confirm both printings appear in Table 1 with the dual-sourced note. Confirm that the admitted fraction is left undefined on the instrument denominator for these rows, for the reason given above. Then move to waves 2 to 4, which can be checked against the instrument alone.

What is missing should be stated plainly, because a reproduction-minded reader deserves the shape of the gap rather than a softened version of it. The raw worker outputs for wave 1 were not retained. Had they been kept, the two counts could have been compared output by output, and one source, or some accounting across both, would have been vindicated. Because they are gone, the honest statement of the record is this: wave 1 has two produced-byte counts that disagree by about a factor of eight, there is no surviving artefact that can adjudicate between them, and the paper prints both numbers, marks the row dual-sourced, and routes every quantitative claim around the gap rather than through it. Every part of that statement is checkable from what survives, and waves 2 to 4 can be verified in full, without the missing outputs ever needing to exist.

AB-S3-OK
